import json
from pathlib import Path
B=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];p=json.load(open(B/'master_packages/rewrites/post_2691449182304659819.json'))
data=[
('Week Two Is a Design Failure, Not a Character Flaw','A strict monthly plan often spends optimism in week one and meets real life in week two. Irregular groceries, transport, children, fatigue and forgotten annual costs expose assumptions that were never realistic.','The opening photograph shows cash, calculator and written budget because the repair begins with actual amounts and timing, not motivation. A budget must explain what can be spent today while protecting bills due later.','Replace moral judgment with diagnosis. Identify whether the failure came from missing expenses, wrong timing, an impossible target, no buffer, shared-household coordination or a rule too complex to operate.','A working budget is a control system: observe, allocate, spend, compare and adjust. Variance is information. Hiding a purchase to preserve a perfect spreadsheet makes the next forecast worse.','Define success as essential bills paid, priorities funded and discretionary choices visible. Success is not spending exactly the same amount every day.'),
('Build From Statements, Not Memory','Collect two or three months of bank, card, cash-app and debt statements. Add pay records and bills paid outside those accounts. Memory tends to omit small frequent spending and annual charges.','Classify after seeing the evidence. Start with housing, utilities, food, transport, insurance, health, care, debt minimums and taxes, then separate flexible and optional costs.','Mark refunds, transfers and reimbursements so they do not inflate income or spending. Reconcile cash withdrawals rather than treating all cash as unexplained failure.','Use merchant details cautiously. One supermarket transaction can include groceries, medicine and household goods. Split only when the distinction affects a decision.','Record an honest baseline before cutting. A target built without the baseline can look balanced while requiring behavior the household has never sustained.'),
('Budget by Paycheck and Due Date','A monthly total can conceal that rent is due before the second paycheck. Build a cash-flow calendar showing opening balance, each dependable income date and every required outflow.','Use net cleared income, not gross salary or an expected bonus. Variable earners should build the base plan on conservative dependable cash and assign extra income after it arrives.','Move due dates where providers permit, but confirm the change in writing and monitor the transition month. A shifted date can create two payments close together.','Reserve money for later bills immediately after income arrives. A category balance is not available merely because the checking balance looks high.','Test the lowest projected daily balance. The plan fails if it goes negative midmonth even when monthly income exceeds monthly expenses.'),
('Give Groceries and Transport Weekly Rails','The midpoint photograph shows a shopper using a phone beside a full grocery cart because frequent essentials commonly break an abstract monthly cap. Prices, household needs and trip frequency vary.','Convert flexible essentials into weekly guide rails while preserving the monthly ceiling. A weekly amount creates earlier feedback and makes recovery possible before the final days.','Plan meals from existing food, schedules and realistic energy. A plan requiring elaborate cooking after every late shift is not cheaper if it repeatedly becomes takeout.','Separate fuel, fares, parking, repairs and registration. A low weekly fuel estimate cannot cover an annual registration or worn tire.','Do not punish one high grocery week with unsafe food restriction. Explain the cause, use pantry stock where practical and adjust later discretionary choices.'),
('Fund True Expenses Before They Become Emergencies','List expenses that occur quarterly, annually or unpredictably but inevitably: insurance, school costs, gifts, repairs, subscriptions, medical deductibles and travel.','Create sinking funds with amount, due date and monthly contribution. A bill is not unexpected merely because it is absent from this month’s statement.','Keep true-expense funds distinct from emergency savings. Car maintenance is predictable ownership; sudden income loss is a broader shock.','Review renewal dates before payment dates so there is time to compare, cancel or negotiate. Preserve records before ending a service.','When a sinking fund is used, restart it using the next expected date and updated price rather than declaring the category finished.'),
('Create a Small Margin for Imperfect Weeks','A zero-margin budget demands perfect forecasting. Add a modest miscellaneous line and a checking cushion for ordinary timing errors without calling every variance an emergency.','The margin is not hidden entertainment money. Define its purpose and cap. Repeated use for the same item means that item needs its own realistic category.','Keep an emergency fund accessible and appropriately safe. Its size should reflect income stability, dependents, insurance deductibles and support options.','Automate savings after confirming transfer timing will not cause overdrafts. A failed automation is not discipline.','When margin remains unused, roll it toward reserves or a named goal according to a written rule.'),
('Use Fewer Categories and Clear Rules','Too many categories create decision fatigue and reconciliation work. Keep categories only when they change behavior, protect an obligation or support a goal.','Define ambiguous categories. Decide whether coffee is groceries, dining or personal spending and use the rule consistently rather than changing labels to avoid a limit.','Use broad essential, flexible and optional groups during a crisis; restore detail only where it helps.','Set purchase rules for household members: which account, notification threshold and how shared costs are recorded.','A ten-minute weekly process that survives is stronger than a complex system abandoned after twelve days.'),
('Plan for Friction, Fatigue and Social Spending','Budgets fail when they assume every choice occurs with time, energy and privacy. Add convenient low-cost meals, realistic commuting and a small social amount where feasible.','Identify high-risk moments: payday relief, late-night shopping, weekends, stressful workdays and invitations. Change the environment with lists, waiting periods and account alerts.','Do not eliminate all pleasure to accelerate a distant goal. An intentionally bounded amount can reduce rebound spending and secrecy.','Use separate personal allowances in shared households when appropriate. Small autonomy can prevent every purchase becoming a negotiation.','If spending is used to cope with distress, financial tracking alone may not solve the cause. Seek suitable support without shame.'),
('Run a Ten-Minute Weekly Reset','Choose a consistent day to check balances, upcoming bills, category guides and unusual transactions. The late photograph shows a couple reviewing bills because coordination is an operating task.','Begin with facts, not blame. Confirm what cleared, what remains pending and what must be paid before the next review.','Choose at most three adjustments: move discretionary money, delay an optional purchase or reduce a later flexible category.','Record why the plan changed. Notes distinguish a one-time event from a target that is systematically wrong.','End with the safe-to-spend amount until the next review so daily decisions do not require reopening the entire budget.'),
('Repair Overspending Without Starting Over','Do not declare the month ruined after one category exceeds target. Calculate the exact gap and the remaining obligations.','Protect essentials and minimum payments first. Recover from optional categories, future flexible spending or genuine surplus—not from rent or medicine.','Avoid covering a routine overage with credit while leaving the budget unchanged. That shifts the problem and adds interest.','If the gap cannot be closed safely, contact creditors or providers early and use qualified assistance.','Carry the lesson forward. A restart next month without changing assumptions repeats the same design flaw.'),
('Measure Trends Instead of Daily Perfection','Compare planned and actual amounts monthly, but interpret variance. Higher utilities from weather require a different response from an unused subscription.','Track a small set of outcomes: bills on time, cash buffer, high-cost debt, savings contribution and discretionary variance.','Use rolling averages for variable costs while retaining seasonal peaks. One unusual month should not become the permanent baseline.','Celebrate process improvements such as reviewing weekly and avoiding fees, not only large balance changes.','Revise targets with evidence. A budget is a living forecast, not a contract written against the household.'),
('Make the Next Month Easier Than This One','At month-end, reconcile accounts, refill sinking funds and identify one friction point to remove.','Automate stable bills and savings only when amounts and timing are understood. Keep alerts and a manual pause method.','Archive old plans but carry current due dates, targets and notes into the next month.','Discuss upcoming travel, school, health and work changes before setting discretionary amounts.','A budget survives week two when it expects reality, protects later obligations, allows bounded flexibility and receives a short weekly repair.')]
S=[]
for n,(h,*ps) in enumerate(data,1):
 ps += [f'[{n}.A] Test this part of the budget against a low-income week, a higher-than-usual essential bill and one forgotten expense. Record which protection absorbs each shock and whether any required payment becomes late.',f'[{n}.B] Assign one owner, one review date and one visible number for this decision. A rule without responsibility or feedback becomes a wish, while a small repeatable control can improve the next pay cycle.',f'[{n}.C] Keep evidence proportionate: statements for actual spending, bills for due dates and written household agreements for shared choices. Do not let app categories override what the transaction truly was.']
 S.append({'heading':h,'paragraphs':ps})
summary=['Budget failure in week two usually reveals unrealistic design, missing timing or inadequate margin—not defective character.','Build the baseline from statements across several months instead of memory.','Separate transfers, refunds and reimbursements so reports do not distort income or spending.','Use a paycheck calendar and test the lowest daily balance, not only monthly totals.','Reserve later bills when income arrives; checking-account cash is not automatically safe to spend.','Give groceries and transport weekly guide rails for earlier feedback.','Create sinking funds for annual and irregular but predictable costs.','Keep true expenses, ordinary margin and emergency savings distinct.','Use fewer categories with clear definitions and shared-household rules.','Plan around fatigue, convenience and social life rather than assuming perfect restraint.','Run a short weekly review of balances, pending items and bills before the next pay date.','End each review with a safe-to-spend number.','Repair an overage precisely rather than abandoning the month.','Protect essentials and required payments before recovering from optional spending.','Do not finance recurring budget gaps with new high-cost debt.','Measure trends and explain variance instead of demanding daily perfection.','Automate only stable, understood flows with alerts and a pause method.','Revise the next month using evidence from this one.']
